\n\n**Reason:**  \n- **N: d5-e7** is a direct fork of king and rook. Black’s king is on f7, rook on h6; the knight can’t be captured because it’s defended by queen on a6. If black moves king, we take the rook on h6. That wins material and forces black into a bad position.  \n- Other moves like c5-c6 are also strong, but the knight fork is more forcing and decisive. It also disrupts black’s coordination.  \n- Black’s bishop on g8 and rook on h6 are not defending e7; pawn on f6 is absent, so e7 is vulnerable.\n\n**Answer:** N: d5-e7"}
