e active diagonal and avoids potential pawn attack if black pushes d5 later.\n\nReason:  \n**B: b5-a6** looks strong because:  \n- It directly attacks the black pawn on b4 (defended only by the rook on d4 and possibly the bishop on b4 is pinned).  \n- If black moves the rook, the bishop on b4 hangs. If black plays ...Rxb4, then Bxb4 leaves black’s pawn structure shattered.  \n- It also clears b5 for a possible knight or other piece, while keeping pressure along the diagonal toward the black king.  \n- This move is more forcing than others, potentially gaining material or positional advantage.\n\nAnswer: B: b5-a6"}
