k moves, then c6 pawn is advanced and supported by b7 bishop? Not directly, but b7 bishop is on same diagonal? c6 is not on that diagonal. But c6 pawn is passed and can promote with support.  \n\nReason: I think **P: c5-c6** is the most forcing move, immediately attacking the black rook and gaining space. Black will likely have to move the rook (maybe to c8 or somewhere else), which then allows White to possibly double rooks on the 7th rank or continue advancing the pawn. Also, pushing the c-pawn creates a passed pawn that could become a queen. Since White is already up material, creating another passed pawn will make the win easier. Alternatives like R: a8-a7 are also good, but c6 is more direct and disruptive.  \n\nI’ll choose P: c5-c6.\n\nAnswer: P: c5-c6'}
