t of squares with pieces; others are empty. Given valid moves only involve White pieces from certain squares, likely Black\'s remaining pieces are not threatening immediately.  \n\nEvaluation:  \n- Material: White seems to have an extra knight and possibly more pawns, given Black only shows king + bishop.  \n- Knight on g7 is deep in Black\'s camp but not directly attacking anything except maybe fork opportunities.  \n- Bishop on b7 is targeting long diagonal toward Black’s king area.  \n- Black\'s king is on d7, somewhat exposed.  \n- Pawn moves: b4-b5 or c4-c5 can advance pawns; f2-f4 or f2-f3 opens possibilities; h3-h4 advances.  \n- King moves: g1-f1, g1-g2, g1-h1 are safe king moves but not urgent.  \n- Bishop b7 has many moves, but most are not immediate threats except maybe b7-d5 attacking e5 bishop? Actually b7-d5 attacks Black’s bishop on e5 (if Black bishop is on e5), and also checks Black’s king? Wait, from b7 to d5: that\'s diagonal b7-c6-d5? No, b7 to d5 is diagonal b7-c6-d5, but d5 is not adjacent to d7 king. It does attack e5 bishop though.  \n- Knight g7 moves: g7-e6 attacks d7 king and e5 bishop simultaneously (fork possibility). g7-f5 also attacks d7? f5 to d7 is knight move? No, f5 to d7 is two squares down and one left? Actually knight from f5 attacks d6 and e7, not d7. So g7-e6 is a double attack: threatens king (check) and bishop.  \n\nPromising moves:  \n1. N: g7-e6 – This is a **knight fork**: attacking Black king (check) and Black bishop on e5. Black must move king out of check, then White captures bishop e5 next move, winning a piece. That seems very strong.  \n2. B: b7-d5 – Attacks bishop e5, but not a check, so Black could move bishop or defend. Less forcing.  \n3. P: b4-b5 or c4-c5 – Pawn advances, but not as forcing.  \n4. P: f2-f4 – Gains space, but not tactical blow.  \n\nBetween these, N: g7-e6 is clearly best because it’s a check and a fork, winning material directly.  \n\nReason: Forcing move that wins a bishop for free (since after check, Black king moves, knight takes bishop). That increases material advantage decisively. Also knight is already deep; moving it to e6 is safe as Black cannot capture it (king must move).  \n\nAnswer: N: g7-e6'}
