* because it attacks d7, a square adjacent to black king, potentially starting to restrict it.  \n\n**Reason:**  \nSince Black only has a king, our main task is to coordinate king and bishop to trap it in a corner of opposite color to bishop’s diagonal. Currently bishop is on white square (b7 is a black square? wait b7 is dark square). Our bishop is on dark squares (b7, a6, c8 are dark). Black king on e7 (dark square). The bishop can harass it but cannot force mate without our king. So best is to move king immediately toward center to help. The move **K: g1-f1** starts bringing king toward e-file/d-file, but it’s slow.  \nAlternatively, **B: b7-c8** eyes d7, which if black king moves to d6, our king can approach. However, black king can go to f8/f7 after Bc8.  \nActually, **B: b7-a6** might be better — it controls c8 and a8, and black king cannot go to c8, useful if we later push king up a-file.  \n\nBut given the valid moves, one immediate idea: **B: b7-f3** puts bishop on long diagonal a8-h1, covering d5, e4, f3, g2, h1. That might be less effective since king can escape e7–f6–g5. Let’s check **B: b7-d5**: d5 is a light square (bishop is dark-squared? Actually b7 to d5 is dark square? b7=dark, d5=light?? No, b7 and d5 are both dark squares. Yes, correct.) So Bd5 attacks e4, f3, g2, h1, c6, b7, a8, etc.  \n\nBut maybe the best move is to start king march: **K: g1-f1** is safe and starts centralizing.  \n\nSince black has no pieces, we can afford to slowly improve our position.  \n\nI choose **K: g1-f1** — it’s solid, centralizes the king, avoids stalemate accidents, and prepares to join the bishop for mating net.  \n\n**Answer:** K: g1-f1'}
