ing to rush to a7 or b8 to try to stop promotion, but White can then use king or bishop to shield the pawn. The bishop can move next turn to a8 or b7 to support promotion. No other move creates such an immediate winning threat. Black’s king is on e7, so it would take at least 3 moves to get to b8 (e7-d6-c7-b8), giving White time to protect.  \n\nTherefore, **P: b6-b7** is the most forcing winning move.  \n\n**Answer:** P: b6-b7'}
